\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The exact pure-gauge torus law}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [square torus](fdip-006M), take #{G=\{1,-1\}} in its
one-dimensional sign representation and use the
[counting convention](fdip-006O). With #{n=|V|=P} and #{\beta\ge0},
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Z(\beta)
 &=2^{n+1}\,\frac{(2\cosh\beta)^P+(2\sinh\beta)^P}{2},\\
Z_W(\beta)&=e^{-\beta P}\mathcal Z(\beta).
\end{aligned}}
Let #{C} be a simple contractible edge loop bounding a specified disk
of #{A} square faces, where #{0<A<P}. Put #{t=\tanh\beta}. Its
Wilson loop is #{W(C)=U_C\in\{1,-1\}}, and
##{\langle W(C)\rangle_W
 =\frac{t^A+t^{P-A}}{1+t^P}.}
The finite formula includes the complementary region through the
second term.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{u_p=U_p}. By the [plaquette counting lemma](fdip-006Q),
every pattern with #{\prod_pu_p=1} has #{2^{n+1}} preimages. The
indicator of this constraint is #{(1+\prod_pu_p)/2}. Therefore
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Z(\beta)
 &=\frac{2^{n+1}}2\sum_{u\in\{1,-1\}^{\mathcal P}}
   \left(1+\prod_pu_p\right)\prod_p e^{\beta u_p}\\
 &=\frac{2^{n+1}}2
   \left[(2\cosh\beta)^P+(2\sinh\beta)^P\right].
\end{aligned}}
Here each unweighted one-sign sum is #{2\cosh\beta} and each
sign-weighted sum is #{2\sinh\beta}. The displayed Wilson action
constant gives the formula for #{Z_W}.}

\p{Multiplying the plaquette signs inside the chosen disk cancels every
interior edge twice and leaves exactly the boundary product, so
#{W(C)=\prod_{p\text{ inside }C}u_p}. Insert this product into the
preceding constrained sum. The two terms in the unnormalized numerator,
after removing the common factor #{2^{n+1}/2}, are
##{\begin{aligned}
 &(2\sinh\beta)^A(2\cosh\beta)^{P-A},\\
 &(2\cosh\beta)^A(2\sinh\beta)^{P-A}.
\end{aligned}}
Divide by the corresponding partition function and then by
#{(2\cosh\beta)^P}. The common action constant cancels, proving the
expectation formula.}
}

\p{Fix #{\beta>0} and a finite disk loop while both torus periods
grow, so #{A} stays fixed and #{P\to\infty}. Then #{0<t<1}, giving
##{\begin{aligned}
\lim_{P\to\infty}\langle W(C)\rangle_W
 &=t^A=e^{-\sigma A},\\
\sigma&=-\log(\tanh\beta)>0.
\end{aligned}}
This is an exact area law for this two-dimensional pure-gauge limit.
It is not a claim for a matter-coupled law or for higher-dimensional
lattices. At #{\beta=0}, the finite formula instead gives zero for
these nonempty proper disk loops, and #{Z_W(0)=2^{2n}}.}
